{"availability":"available","full":"saintparish4/opal","name":"opal","description":"An all-in-one JavaScript toolkit in a single Rust binary, built on one shared module graph. Today: opal install, a beta npm-compatible package manager with a content-addressed store, crash-safe installs, and resolution cross-checked against npm.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Rust","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2026-08-18T22:21:42Z","updated":"2026-10-02T18:10:24Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://saintparish4.github.io/opal/","topics":[],"demo":null,"color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Rust"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Opal is an all-in-one toolkit for JavaScript and TypeScript projects, built to cover package management, running code, bundling, and testing. It ships as a single executable called opal.","At its core is opal-core, a module graph engine written in Rust. It works out what every file imports and where each import resolves, then caches that answer by content hash, so the next run over unchanged files is a cache hit. Every tool Opal adds is meant to share that one graph instead of carrying its own resolver.","The first of those tools is the package manager, and it works today. It picks the versions npm would, with a few documented differences, lays out nodemodules the way npm does, and links every file in from a single content-addressed store shared by all the projects on your machine."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/saintparish4/opal/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"30d615c610e4d004b25339e03416bace913c34c4bb4b11c926428ff6dd77ec60","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T09:36:58.563Z",